"On the multipeakon system of a two-component Novikov equation - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167278925002581)\nciteturn7search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # On the multipeakon system of a two-component Novikov equation ... Other papers that completely changed our understanding of integrability in physical models were papers of Zakharov and his collaborators on the Einstein equations [3], the self-dual Yang–Mills theory [4], [5], two-dimensional field theory [6], and culminating in the treatise [7]. ... One of the goals of the present paper is to understand the transition from a scalar peakon equation to a multi-component peakon system, with the aim of ultimately classifying the peakon equations.\n\n# On the multipeakon system of a two-component Novikov equation\n\nDedicated to the memory of V.E. Zakharov\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Xiang-Ke Chang ^{a} ^{b}, Jacek Szmigielski ^{c}\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.physd.2025.134781\n\nAccess through your organizationPurchase PDF\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article preview\n  * Recommended articles\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe Novikov equation is a fascinating integrable modification of the Camassa–Holm equation with remarkable properties. This paper concentrates on a two-component Novikov equation featuring a non-self-adjoint 4 × 4 Lax operator. We explore the associated forward and inverse spectral maps, as well as global existence and long-time asymptotics concerning the peakon sector. On one hand, we execute an isospectral deformation to the long-time regime to compute the relevant eigenvalues using a Moser-inspired technique. To achieve this, we first demonstrate the global existence of the peakon flows, then establish a connection between the long-time asymptotics of positions and momenta and the non-zero eigenvalues. We introduce a trio of Weyl functions and show that they are matrix-valued Stieltjes transforms of discrete positive measures, again employing the deformation technique. This effectively linearizes the peakon flows on the spectral data side. Conversely, we tackle the inverse problem using Hermite–Padé approximation techniques that involve tensor products and an added symmetry condition. The thorough analysis guarantees existence and uniqueness, yielding global multipeakon formulas.\n\n## MSC\n\n37K15\n\n;\n\n37K40\n\n;\n\n34A55\n\n;\n\n34B09\n\n;\n\n41A28\n\n## Keywords\n\nForward and inverse spectral problems\n\n;\n\nMultipeakons\n\n;\n\nNovikov equation\n\n;\n\nHermite–padé approximations\n\n;\n\nLong time asymptotics\n\n## Introduction\n\nThis paper is dedicated to the memory of Prof. V.E. Zakharov. His work has profoundly influenced many generations of mathematical physicists, including the two authors of the present work. It is hard to imagine how different the theory of integrable systems would have been without the monumental contribution of Zakharov and Shabat’s 1972 paper [1], followed by [2]. These are works that any student of the modern theory of integrable systems must study before undertaking research work. Other papers that completely changed our understanding of integrability in physical models were papers of Zakharov and his collaborators on the Einstein equations [3], the self-dual Yang–Mills theory [4], [5], two-dimensional field theory [6], and culminating in the treatise [7].\n\nThe second author had the privilege of discussing the topic of the Camassa–Holm equation with Prof. Zakharov, both in Tucson, AZ, USA, and Bialowieża, Poland. The main point of the discussion centered around integrable systems with discrete spectrum only, no continuous spectrum. The Camassa–Holm equation with the zero linear dispersion and spatially decaying data on both sides belongs to that class, and so does the Toda lattice [8]. It has been one of the pinnacles of the theory of integrable systems, stated already in Gardner, Kruskal and Miura [9], as well as Lax’s paper [10] and, also, in Zakharov and Shabat [1], that integrable equations originate from isospectral problems. Before the Camassa–Holm equation, the class of isospectral problems associated with operators having purely discrete spectra had not been particularly studied, apart from the flurry of activities surrounding the Toda lattice in the 1970s. However, there was an exception. In a remarkable book by Pöschl and Trubowitz [11], published around 1987, the authors thoroughly examined the isospectral manifold associated with the Schrödinger-type Sturm–Liouville problem on the finite interval -{u}^{\\prime \\prime }+qu=\\lambda u,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16667em}{0ex}}u\\left(0\\right)=0=u\\left(1\\right),q\\in {L}^{2}\\left([0,1]\\right)$-{u}^{\\prime \\prime }+qu=\\lambda u,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16667em}{0ex}}u\\left(0\\right)=0=u\\left(1\\right),q\\in {L}^{2}\\left([0,1]\\right)$. There was no follow-up on that work at the time. Later, it became apparent that the Camassa–Holm equation belongs to the same class; it is an isospectral deformation of a classical, non-homogeneous string -{u}^{\\prime \\prime }=\\lambda \\rho u,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16667em}{0ex}}u\\left(0\\right)=0=u\\left(1\\right),\\rho \\in \\mathcal{M}\\left([0,1]\\right)$-{u}^{\\prime \\prime }=\\lambda \\rho u,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16667em}{0ex}}u\\left(0\\right)=0=u\\left(1\\right),\\rho \\in \\mathcal{M}\\left([0,1]\\right)$, with the measure-valued density \\rho $\\rho $. The second author is unsure whether he convinced Prof. Zakharov of the importance of the Camassa–Holm equation for the complete landscape of integrable systems. In a way, this work aims to continue those discussions. In the body of the paper, we will refer to Camassa–Holm type equations as peakon equations to emphasize that the presence of non-smooth solitons is the unifying feature of all these equations. One of the goals of the present paper is to understand the transition from a scalar peakon equation to a multi-component peakon system, with the aim of ultimately classifying the peakon equations. This will be elaborated on more in-depth in the subsequent sections of the introduction.\n\n### Multipeakons\n\nThis section presents concrete examples of global multipeakons written in terms of the spectral data for the NV2 equation (1.9). To facilitate the presentation of formulas, we will make a few extra assumptions about the determinants involving the spectral data {\\{{\\lambda }_{k},{\\mathbf{b}}_{k}\\}}_{k=1}^{N}\\in \\mathcal{S}\\left(N\\right)${\\{{\\lambda }_{k},{\\mathbf{b}}_{k}\\}}_{k=1}^{N}\\in \\mathcal{S}\\left(N\\right)$. Thus, for the remainder of this section, we will assume that {F}_{k}^{(0,0)}>0,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16667em}{0ex}}{F}_{k}^{(1,1)}>0,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16667em}{0ex}}{F}_{k}^{(1,0)}>0,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16667em}{0ex}}{\\mathbf{G}}_{k}^{(1,0)}>0.${F}_{k}^{(0,0)}>0,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16667em}{0ex}}{F}_{k}^{(1,1)}>0,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16667em}{0ex}}{F}_{k}^{(1,0)}>0,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16667em}{0ex}}{\\mathbf{G}}_{k}^{(1,0)}>0.$\n\nWith conditions (8.1) in place we can rewrite (7.16) in an equivalent form, namely, {x}_{N-k+1}=\\frac{1}{2}ln\\left(\\frac{2{F}_{k}^{(0,0)}}{{F}_{k-1}^{(1,1)}}\\right),\\phantom{\\rule{2em}{0ex}}{\\mathbf{m}}_{N-k+1}=${x}_{N-k+1}=\\frac{1}{2}ln\\left(\\frac{2{F}_{k}^{(0,0)}}{{F}_{k-1}^{(1,1)}}\\right),\\phantom{\\rule{2em}{0ex}}{\\mathbf{m}}_{N-k+1}=$\n\n### CRediT authorship contribution statement\n\nXiang-Ke Chang: Investigation, Writing – review & editing. Jacek Szmigielski: Investigation, Writing – review & editing.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the multipeakon system of a two-component Novikov equation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.12221)\nciteturn7academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.9 years ago; Title: On the multipeakon system of a two-component Novikov equation\nTitle: On the multipeakon system of a two-component Novikov equation\nAuthors: Xiang-Ke Chang, Jacek Szmigielski\nDate: Tue Dec 19 15:10:08 2023\n\nWe are exploring variations of the Novikov equation that have weak solutions called peakons. Our focus is on a two-component Novikov equation with a non-self-adjoint $4\\times 4$ Lax operator for which we examine the related forward and inverse spectral maps for the peakon sector. To tackle the forward spectral problem, we convert it into a matrix eigenvalue problem, for which the original boundary value problem is a two-fold cover. We then use an isospectral deformation to the long-time regime to calculate the relevant eigenvalues. To support the long-term deformation, we prove the global existence of the peakon flows using ideas based on Moser's deformation method, which was used in his study of the finite Toda lattice. We subsequently solve the inverse problem by studying a trio of Weyl functions that can be approximated simultaneously by rational functions that involve tensor products and an additional symmetry condition. This part of our paper is rooted in Krein's solution to the inverse problem for the Stieltjes string.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTRANSITIVE AND GALLAI COLORINGS (https://pages.uoregon.edu/arkadiy/transitive.pdf)\nciteturn7search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.1 years ago; TRANSITIVE AND GALLAI COLORINGS ... Conjecture 5.4 was checked for n ≤6. ... The type B Catalan number CB ... Proof.\nTRANSITIVE AND GALLAI COLORINGS\n25\nConjecture 5.4. The number of maximal transitive partitions of the set Φ+(Bn) of positive roots\nof type Bn is\nn\nX\n\u0013\n.\nCB\nn :=\n3k + 1\nn + k + 1\n\u00122n −k\nn −2k\nk=0\nConjecture 5.4 was checked for n ≤6.\nRemark 5.5. The type B Catalan number CB\nn−1 from Conjecture 5.4 is equal to the number of\nordered pairs (α, β) of compositions of n with the same number of parts, such that\nrP\ni=1\nαi ̸=\nrP\ni=1\nβi\n(∀r < #parts); see [29, A081696] and [5, Theorem 1.1]. Note that the number of maximal transitive\npartitions of type An−1 is equal to the 