onal biology is simple: phylogenetic trees are precisely trees with this structure. We begin by defining several notions from chemical graph theory. The *Wiener index* [40] of a tree is the sum of pairwise hop-distances between nodes in the tree. I.e., for a tree \\(T\\),\n\n\\[\nW(T)=\\sum_{u,v\\in V(T)} d_T(u,v).\n\\]\n\nSince its introduction in 1947, this has been one of the most widely used metrics in the study of quantitative structure-activity relationships in chemistry. See [10] for a thorough survey.\n\nJoCG 16(1), 1–34, 2025  13--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe type-a0B transitive-partition enumeration conjecture | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/360000/the-type-a0b-transitive-partition-enumeration-conjecture)\nciteturn7search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; A type-$B_n$B n​ transitive-partition enumeration conjecture. ... Roichman, “Transitive and Gallai colorings”, arXiv:2309.11203 (2023).\n\nThe type-\\a0B transitive-partition enumeration conjecture\n\nLet $B_n$B n​ be the Coxeter system of type $B_n$B n​, and let $\\Phi^+(B_n)$Φ+(B n​) denote its set of positive roots. A type-$B_n$B n​ transitive-partition enumeration conjecture. The number of maximal transitive partitions of $\\Phi^+(B_n)$Φ+(B n​) is\n\n$$C^B_n:= \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\frac{3k+1}{n+k+1} \\binom{2n-k}{n-2k}.$$C n B​:=k=0∑n​n+k+1 3 k+1​(n−2 k 2 n−k​).\n\nThis proposes an explicit type-$B$B analogue of the preceding enumeration results for dihedral groups and type $A$A; the source gives no resolution evidence, so the conjecture remains open.\n\n## Primary source\n\nR. M. Adin, A. Berenstein, J. Greenstein, J. -R. Li, A. Marmor and Y. Roichman, “Transitive and Gallai colorings”, arXiv:2309.11203 (2023).\n\ncoxeter-groupsroot-systemstransitive-colorings\n\n0 0\n\n3 weeks ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTransitive and Gallai colorings (https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.11203)\nciteturn7academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.1 years ago; We count Gallai and transitive colorings of the root system of type A using the maximal number of colors, and show that, when equipped with a natural descent set map, the resulting quasisymmetric function is symmetric and Schur-positive.\nTitle: Transitive and Gallai colorings\nAuthors: R. M. Adin, A. Berenstein, J. Greenstein, J. -R. Li, A. Marmor, Y. Roichman\nDate: Wed Sep 20 10:38:17 2023\n\nA Gallai coloring of the complete graph is an edge-coloring with no rainbow triangle. This concept first appeared in the study of comparability graphs and anti-Ramsey theory. We introduce a transitive analogue for acyclic directed graphs, and generalize both notions to Coxeter systems, matroids and commutative algebras. It is shown that for any finite matroid (or oriented matroid), the maximal number of colors is equal to the matroid rank. This generalizes a result of Erd\\H{o}s-Simonovits-S\\'os for complete graphs. The number of Gallai (or transitive) colorings of the matroid that use at most $k$ colors is a polynomial in $k$. Also, for any acyclic oriented matroid, represented over the real numbers, the number of transitive colorings using at most 2 colors is equal to the number of chambers in the dual hyperplane arrangement. We count Gallai and transitive colorings of the root system of type A using the maximal number of colors, and show that, when equipped with a natural descent set map, the resulting quasisymmetric function is symmetric and Schur-positive.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn Steiner Trees of the regular simplex | Journal of Computational Geometry (https://jocg.org/index.php/jocg/article/view/4741)\nciteturn7search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Crawled: last month; # On Steiner Trees of the regular simplex ... Motivated by this connection, we investigate optimal Steiner trees of regular simplices, proving new structural properties of their optimal Steiner trees, revisiting an old conjecture of Smith [Algorithmica'92] about their optimal topology, and providing the first explicit, general construction of candidate optimal Steiner trees for that topology.\nMoreover, the explicit construction of optimal Steiner trees remains unknown for almost all well-studied high-dimensional point configurations. Furthermore, a vast majority the state-of-the-art structural results on (high-dimensional) Euclidean Steiner trees were established in the 1960s, with no noteworthy update in over half a century.\n\nIn this paper, we revisit high-dimensional Euclidean Steiner trees, proving new structural results. We also establish a link between the computational hardness of the Euclidean Steiner Tree problem and understanding the optimal Steiner trees of regular simplices (and simplicial complexes), proposing several conjectures and showing that some of them suffice to resolve the status of the inapproximability of the Euclidean Steiner Tree problem. Motivated by this connection, we investigate optimal Steiner trees of regular simplices, proving new structural properties of their optimal Steiner trees, revisiting an old conjecture of Smith [Algorithmica'92] about their optimal topology, and providing the first explicit, general construction of candidate optimal Steiner trees for that topology.\n\n### Downloads\n\nDownload data is not yet available.\n\n## Downloads\n\n  * PDF\n\n## Published\n\n2025-02-19\n\n## How to Cite\n\nFleischmann, Henry, Guillermo Gamboa Quintero, Karthik C. S., Josef Matějka, and Jakub Petr. 2025. “On Steiner Trees of the Regular Simplex”. Journal of Computational Geometry 16 (1):1–34. https://doi.org/10.20382/jocg.v16i1a1.\n\n## Issue\n\nVol. 16 No. 1 (2025)\n\n## Section\n\nArticles--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTransitive and Gallai colorings of the complete graph (https://pages.uoregon.edu/arkadiy/transitive.gallai.complete.graphs.pdf)\nciteturn7search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; **Conjecture 5.5. ... (b) For all \\(n > 1\\) and \\(k \\ge 1\\), ... Transitive 2-colorings and Orlik-Terao algebras ... For the sake of simplicity, we will only discuss the case of the reflection hyperplane arrangement of type \\(A_{n-1}\\).\nR.M. Adin, A. Berenshtein, J. Greenstein et al.  \nEuropean Journal of Combinatorics 130 (2025) 104225\n\nFor the Hilbert series of the transitive algebra \\(T_{n,2}\\) see Corollary 5.12 below.\n\n**Conjecture 5.5.** Let \\([k]_j := \\prod_{i=0}^{j-1}\\frac{q^{k-i-1}}{q-1}\\) \\((k,j \\ge 1)\\), the \\(q\\)-analogue of the falling factorial.\n\n(a) For all \\(n > 1\\) and \\(k \\ge 1\\),\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Hilb}(T_{n,k}, q)=\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} P_{n,j}(q)\\cdot [k]_j,\n\\]\nwhere \\(P_{n,1}(q),\\ldots,P_{n,n-1}(q)\\in \\mathbb{Z}_{\\ge 0}[q]\\). The leading coefficient satisfies \\(P_{n,n-1}(q)=q^{\\binom{n-1}{2}}c_{n-1}\\), where \\(c_{n-1}\\) is the Catalan number.\n\n(b) For all \\(n > 1\\) and \\(k \\ge 1\\),\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Hilb}(G_{n,k}, q)=\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} Q_{n,j}(q)\\cdot [k]_j,\n\\]\nwhere \\(Q_{n,1}(q),\\ldots,Q_{n,n-1}(q)\\in \\mathbb{Z}_{\\ge 0}[q]\\).\n\nPart (a) was checked for \\(n \\le 8\\). Part (b) was checked for \\(n \\le 5\\).\n\n**Remark 5.6.** For all \\(j\\), \\(P_{n,j}(1)\\) is equal to the number of transitive partitions with \\(j\\) blocks of the edge set of \\(\\bar{K}_n\\), and \\(Q_{n,j}(1)\\) is equal to the number of Galli partitions with \\(j\\) blocks of the edge set of \\(K_n\\).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTransitive and Gallai colorings | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2309.11203)\nciteturn7search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; We count Gallai and transitive colorings of the root system of type A using the maximal number of colors, and show that, when equipped with a natural descent set map, the resulting quasisymmetric function is symmetric and Schur-positive.\nSubmitted 20 Sept 2023\n\n# Transitive and Gallai colorings\n\nBar Ilan University Temple University University of Haifa\n\nRM\n\nR. M. Adin\n\nAB\n\nA. Berenstein\n\nJG\n\nJ. Greenstein\n\nJL\n\nJ.-R. Li\n\nAM\n\nA. Marmor\n\nYR\n\nY. Roichman+1 more\n\n## Abstract\n\nA Gallai coloring of the complete graph is an edge-coloring with no rainbow triangle. This concept first appeared in the study of comparability graphs and anti-Ramsey theory. We introduce a transitive analogue for acyclic directed graphs, and generalize both notions to Coxeter systems, matroids and commutative algebras. It is shown that for any finite matroid (or oriented matroid), the maximal number of colors is equal to the matroid rank. This generalizes a result of Erd\\H{o}s-Simonovits-S\\'os for complete graphs. The number of Gallai (or transitive) colorings of the matroid that use at most $k$k colors is a polynomial in $k$k. Also, for any acyclic oriented matroid, represented over the real numbers, the number of transitive colorings using at most 2 colors is equal to the number of chambers in the dual hyperplane arrangement. We count Gallai and transitive colorings of the root system of type A using the maximal number of colors, and show that, when equipped with a natural descent set map, the resulting quasisymmetric function is symmetric and Schur-positive.\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{adin2023transitivegallaicolorings, title={Transitive and Gallai colorings}, author={R. M. Adin and A. Berenstein and J. Greenstein and J. -R. Li and A. Marmor and Y. Roichman}, year={2023}, eprint={2309.11203}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.11203